**Read:** [Materials Processing — Chris Schuh, MIT 3.044](../sources/Materials-Processing-MIT-3-044.lingua#r_4V5BA7W1TFKGRTGNHN7ZTAK1TW), the complete heat/mass/flow theory and corresponding problem sets; [Principles of Extractive Metallurgy — Tarun Kumar Kundu, NPTEL](../sources/Principles-of-Extractive-Metallurgy-NPTEL.lingua#r_7VCNEHN5VCQDDQTZ512PSHFZ78), the full reaction/transport context for the chosen extraction process; [Metals, alloys, steels, and graduate modeling — Bhadeshia teaching materials](../sources/Metals-Alloys-and-Graduate-Teaching-Bhadeshia.lingua#r_0HZJKN8WC6SEWA0DABM8P4HC3F), the full diffusion and graduate irreversible-process material.

After the shared reaction-versus-transport step, define the local conserved quantity and derive accumulation from fluxes and reaction/source terms. Heat, species, total mass, momentum, and electrical charge have related balance structures but different constitutive relations and constraints. A familiar coefficient cannot be transferred between them without a physical model.

For an appropriate constant-diffusivity concentration model, diffusive flux is J = −D∇c. In nonideal or multicomponent systems, chemical potentials, coupled mobilities, reference frames, and volume/density changes need explicit treatment; a concentration gradient alone does not always identify the flux direction. At an interface, bulk transport, reaction, phase change, and partition may all contribute.

State initial and boundary conditions, dimensional scales, material properties, and what is measured. A surface mass-transfer coefficient is an effective relation for specified geometry/flow/material conditions, not a universal material constant. Separate rate-limiting approximations from a model retaining several comparable resistances.

**Work:** reconstruct a full transfer/reaction problem from an extraction or processing case. Derive the governing balance and boundary relations, nondimensionalize the important terms, and test a limiting case independently. Compare a different plausible transport interpretation with measured profiles or rate data. Explain the observation that distinguishes a real chemical-rate change from a change in mixing, interfacial area, or boundary-layer transport.

**Continue:** [Test heterogeneous reaction mechanisms with geometry, conversion, and independent evidence](Heterogeneous-reaction-shrinking-core-and-mechanism-tests.lingua#r_6CK5EQM4MP5QHJY0AAQXBQYFV7).

**Return:** [Metals and metallurgical production — feed, refining, structure, useful products, and recovery](../Metals-and-metallurgical-production.lingua#r_59BDAQXT81552YF5F8TH4KQQH1).
